{
  "id": "ep146",
  "duration": 48.223,
  "beats": [
    {
      "start": 0.08,
      "end": 4.308,
      "text": "You do not have to wait until 2061 to see Halley's Comet.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Lspn comet halley.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA/W. Liller",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lspn_comet_halley.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 0.0,
          "end": 4.3,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 4.308,
      "end": 11.253,
      "text": "Most people believe the iconic traveler vanished after 1986 and will not return for decades.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Comet Halley and the Milky Way.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA/Ames/Kuiper Airborne Observatory/Lynn Albaugh",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Comet_Halley_and_the_Milky_Way.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 4.3,
          "end": 11.267,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 11.253,
      "end": 17.348,
      "text": "Halley takes about 76 years to orbit the Sun, shedding dust and debris along its path.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Halley's multiple dust tail (eso8605a).jpg",
        "credit": "ESO",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Halley%27s_multiple_dust_tail_(eso8605a).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 11.267,
          "end": 17.333,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 17.348,
      "end": 23.718,
      "text": "Every late October, Earth plows directly through this trail, igniting the Orionid meteor shower.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Orionid Meteor Shower Composite (50521737888).jpg",
        "credit": "Jim Vajda",
        "license": "CC BY 2.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Orionid_Meteor_Shower_Composite_(50521737888).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 17.333,
          "end": 21.8,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 21.8,
          "end": 23.733,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 23.718,
      "end": 29.613,
      "text": "These particles strike the upper atmosphere at a blistering 66 kilometers per second.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Micrometeoroid hole.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Micrometeoroid_hole.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 23.733,
          "end": 26.633,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 26.633,
          "end": 29.6,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 29.613,
      "end": 36.483,
      "text": "That violent friction vaporizes the dust into incandescent trains lingering for seconds to minutes.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Orionid meteor at dawn (54867777518).jpg",
        "credit": "Mike Lewinski",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Orionid_meteor_at_dawn_(54867777518).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 29.6,
          "end": 32.833,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 32.833,
          "end": 36.467,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 36.483,
      "end": 43.058,
      "text": "Halley even produces a second shower, the [Eta Aquariids](/\u02c8It\u02c8\u028c\u02c8\u028ckw\u02c8\u025b\u0279\u02c8i\u02c8Idz/), crossing Earth's path every May.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Meteor shower in the Chilean Desert (potw2227a).jpg",
        "credit": "ESO/P. Horalek",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Meteor_shower_in_the_Chilean_Desert_(potw2227a).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 36.467,
          "end": 43.067,
          "kind": "cover",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 43.058,
      "end": 47.873,
      "text": "The famous traveler's ancient debris is already burning right above your head tonight.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Lspn comet halley.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA/W. Liller",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lspn_comet_halley.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 43.067,
          "end": 48.233,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
